 ## Background
-3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1, S2]. The initial implementation utilized a per-pixel parallelization strategy with atomic operations for gradient accumulation. Subsequent research identified bottlenecks in memory bandwidth and atomic contention, leading to per-splat parallelization strategies [S1]. As scene sizes grew into the billions of Gaussians, single-GPU memory limits became a barrier, prompting distributed frameworks like Grendel-GS [S1, unofficial]. Variants such as MaskGaussian [S1] and FaCT-GS [S3] introduced specialized loss terms and gradient computations to improve reconstruction quality and training efficiency. Implementation details regarding scene extent calculation and half-precision stability were refined to address biases and numerical instability in large-scale urban and indoor scenes [S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
+3D Gaussian Splatting was introduced by Kerbl et al. in 2023 [S1]. The initial implementation used a per-pixel parallel backward pass, which suffered from high atomic contention on GPUs. Subsequent work identified that per-splat parallelization reduces this contention [S1]. The need for distributed training arose as scene sizes grew beyond single-GPU memory capacities, leading to frameworks like Grendel-GS [S1, unofficial]. Variants like MaskGaussian introduced probabilistic masks for better pruning, requiring specialized gradient computations [S1]. Implementation details such as corrected scene extent calculation were identified to prevent bias in Gaussian densification [S3].
 
